            	<h2>What's it about?</h2>
<p><strong>Higher-order Fourier analysis</strong> is an extension of classic Fourier analysis, where one extends the notion of characters to low-degree polynomial phases. This study originated in number theory in the seminal work of Gowers on Szemeredi's theorem and has been extensively developed in the last few years.</p>

<p> Recently, there have been several striking applications of the theory to computer science, which are the focus of this workshop. These include: explicit constructions of functions having low correlation with polynomials, learning structural decompositions of low degree polynomials, list-decoding of Reed-Muller codes, and a unified framework for testing of algebraic properties.</p>

<br/> <br/>

			<h2>Who is it for?</h2>
			<p>The workshop is intended for a general TCS audience, and no familiarity with past work in this area is needed.</p>

<br/> <br />
			
			<h2>When and where is it?</h2>
			<p>It will be held on the tutorial and workshop day for <a href="http://www.boazbarak.org/focs14/">FOCS 2014</a>, October 18, 2014 at the <a href="http://www.radissonblu.com/hotel-philadelphia">conference hotel</a> from 11am to 5:30pm. See the <a href="program.html">program</a> for more details.<p>
			
<br/> <br />
			
			<h2>How do I sign up?</h2>
			<p>Please register at the <a href="http://dimacs.rutgers.edu/FOCS14/Registration_Information/registration_information.html">main FOCS registration site</a>.<p>
			
            
